    "updated_at": "2023-04-17T22:22:32Z",
    "body": "Hi 👋\nI faced the same issue on Ubuntu 22.\n\nThat permission issue seems quite strange to me. Why would a snap binary not be allowed to read and write in the home folder of the user who runs it? I figured it could be due to the OS performing some kind of isolation when executing snaps 🤔 But I may be mistaken, how snaps work is not yet too clear for me 😅 Also I have several other snaps that read and write in the home folder, so that is a quite common pattern.\n\nAlso, the snap binary seemed very slow. With the default configuration, the prompt would take a significant amount of time to display, even when pressing `enter` on an empty command line.\n\nI finally fell back to the `cargo install` approach, which was much more successful :) Cargo is very easy to setup. Make sure `cmake` is installed!",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
